# **The document on screen is not the editor** (ADR-0401). ADR-0396 put eight
# documents behind one screen and left one question asked of the wrong one:
# the shell's quit guard read `EditDirty`, which answers about the document
# being drawn. That was the whole editor before there was a second document,
# and afterwards it was a *silent* loss of work -- type into `a.pas`, open a
# clean `b.pas` over it, press Ctrl-Q once, and the editor took you at your
# word.
#
# Ctrl-Q is the shell's and no session can press it, so what this pins is the
# question the guard now asks. Two claims, and the last line of the golden is
# where both are read:
#
#   - `dirty` and `unsaved` are different answers about the same editor --
#     the session ends on `c.pas`, which was never typed into, so the mark on
#     the status line says nothing and two other documents hold changes;
#   - and `unsaved` counts them rather than answering a boolean, which is
#     what the second dirty document is here for. A count that stopped at the
#     current document reads 0 and a count that stopped at the first one it
#     found reads 1.
#
# `push` fills a buffer the way loading a file does and leaves no mark, which
# is what makes `b.pas` clean until a key is pressed in it.
name a.pas
size 10 30
push program a(output);
keys X
draw
func 3
keys b.pas
ctrl M
draw
push program b(output);
keys Y
draw
func 3
keys c.pas
ctrl M
draw
